 "why": [
  "We profiled CanvasMC with Java Flight Recorder during both 300-player tests. About half of its tick time (53% with the players standing, 50% with them moving) went to the locator bar: the server keeps every player's marker (waypoint) up to date for every other player. With everyone in one place, that work grows with the square of the player count.",
  "We profiled Petal the same way with the 300 players standing. The locator bar does not show up in its profile at all; instead its tick thread spent over a third of its time in the entity tracker (working out which players see which entities and sending them updates) and about a fifth working through each player's queue of packets waiting to be sent. Its network threads were also very busy: about 5 CPU cores and 600 to 800 MB of new memory per second in both 300-player tests, against under one core and under 10 MB per second for Vex.",
  "Vex reworked how the locator bar, the entity tracker and the network code run, so it does the same job with much less work per tick. In the redstone test, part of the difference is the redstone engine (see the + Alternate Current runs); Vex is still faster than both with the same engine."
 ],
 "setup": [
  [
   "PC",
   "AMD Ryzen 7 5700X (8 cores, 16 threads), 32 GB DDR4, Windows 11 Pro"
  ],
  [
   "Java",
   "Oracle Java 25.0.3 with the G1 garbage collector and the same flags for every server: a 6 GB heap in the 300-player tests, an 8 GB heap with Aikar's flags in the redstone, fighting and cow pen tests"
  ],
  [
   "Vex",
   "Vex 26.2 update 47 (13 November 2026)"
  ],
  [
   "CanvasMC",
   "the last Minecraft 26.2 build of CanvasMC (commit 2a3bf65c, 29 September 2026), built from the official source"
  ],
  [
   "Petal",
   "Petal 26.1.2 build 1 (commit 9cc019a, 17 September 2026). Petal runs Minecraft 26.1.2, so its bots connect with the 26.1.2 protocol; the packets they send are the same as on 26.2"
  ],
  [
   "World",
   "a new flat world for every run, made from the same seed and layers (no structures), view distance 10, simulation distance 10"
  ],
  [
   "Settings",
   "each server's own default config, with netty threads set to 8 on all of them. For the redstone test, Vex's redstone clock limiter was turned off: it slows clocks down on purpose, and every server should run exactly the same redstone"
  ],
  [
   "Runs",
   "every test ran twice per server; the page shows the average of the counted runs. The servers took turns test by test on 14 November 2026, the second round in the reverse order. CanvasMC's 300-player runs that lost their bots were tried again at the end (see the fine print)"
  ],
  [
   "Measuring",
   "MSPT and TPS come from Folia's own per-region tick timings (what /tps shows), read by a small measuring plugin once all players are online and the warm-up is over. Vex and CanvasMC run the same plugin jar; Petal runs a build of the same measuring code made for its API, because its older Folia keeps the tick timings in a different package"
  ]
 ],
 "fine": [
  "Measured on a desktop PC, not on a dedicated server. Your own numbers will be different; what matters on this page is the gap between the servers on the same machine.",
  "In the two 300-player tests the bot clients run inside the server's Java process and connect to it over the network like a real game client; their own CPU and memory use is left out of the numbers.",
  "In the two 300-player tests the bots' own logic (reading the world around them to move like a client) runs on the server's threads. What it allocates there is measured on every bot tick and left out of the server's memory numbers, the same way on every server. The redstone, fighting and cow pen tests use a separate bot program.",
  "Vex uses Alternate Current redstone by default (a faster redstone engine that Paper ships as an option); CanvasMC and Petal use vanilla redstone by default. The extra \u201c+ Alternate Current\u201d runs show both of them with that same setting turned on.",
  "Every server ran with its own default settings, including the optimizations it turns on by default. Tuning can improve any of them; these are the numbers you get out of the box.",
  "Every server ran every test with only the bench plugins installed. A real server with many plugins spends extra time in those plugins on any software.",
  "Petal was measured on Minecraft 26.1.2, the version its build supports; Vex and CanvasMC on 26.2.",
  "In the two 300-player tests, CanvasMC's outgoing network data sometimes piled up faster than the bots could read it (they run inside the server's Java process), until it filled the 6 GB of direct memory Java allows by default and the server disconnected the bots. That happened in four of CanvasMC's six 300-player runs; those runs are not counted, so each of its two 300-player results comes from one complete run. Vex and Petal never lost a bot.",
  "Not counted: CanvasMC's 300 players standing run 1 of 14 November, where 283 of the 300 bots were disconnected during the measurement.",
  "Not counted: CanvasMC's 300 players standing run 2 of 14 November, where 279 of the 300 bots were disconnected during the measurement.",
  "Not counted: CanvasMC's 300 players moving run 1 of 14 November, where 281 of the 300 bots were disconnected during the measurement.",
  "Not counted: CanvasMC's 300 players moving run 3 of 14 November, where 280 of the 300 bots were disconnected during the measurement."
 ]
